-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Eviction Policy
+
+The cache uses a 2Q (two-queue) replacement policy to protect repeatedly accessed comparison sheets during long sequential screenings. Entries enter a FIFO admission queue (A1) on first access. An A1 entry evicted before reuse is discarded. An A1 entry accessed again before eviction promotes to an LRU main queue (Am). Am holds sheets proven reusable and evicts by recency when full.
+
+Queue sizing: A1 holds 25% of the 12 GiB budget; Am holds the remaining 75%. A1's smaller size limits scan pollution—a sequential screening of many one-use clips cycles through A1 without displacing Am's comparison working set. Eviction selects from A1 first (FIFO order), then from Am (LRU order) if A1 is empty.
+
+All replacement metadata—queue membership, access timestamps, FIFO position—remains private to the preview worker. The review API, render queue, and deduplication map are unaffected.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (two-queue LRU) with 25% A1, 75% Am split | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q protects the comparison working set (repeatedly accessed sheets) from eviction during long sequential screenings of one-use clips. LRU alone is vulnerable to scans—a screening through thousands of clips evicts useful comparison sheets. Frequency-based aging protects popular clips but adds counter maintenance overhead and can retain yesterday's favorites too long when editors switch projects. The 25/75 split gives A1 enough capacity to absorb typical screening runs without polluting Am. |
